            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The primary meaning is evangelism, the gospel, and the gathering of souls into God's kingdom, with secondary, context-dependent meanings including divine judgment, oppressive conquest, and a trap set by the wicked.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"A unifying concept of an enclosure used by a power to extract the living from the 'sea of peoples' for ultimate sorting and judgment, applying equally to the gospel, God's armies, and the traps of the wicked.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Distinct, context-dependent metaphors primarily denoting evangelistic gathering, but alternatively meaning entrapment or judgment.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"An enclosure by which a power takes the living from the sea of peoples for the sorting of the harvest (consecration or destruction).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_BROADENS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"SAME_CASES\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus treats the gospel, divine judgment, and traps of the wicked as separate, context-dependent symbols. The book unifies them under a single functional definition, arguing they all represent the same mechanism of extracting people for ultimate sorting or devotion.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book accepts the consensus applications (gospel, judgment, traps) but reclassifies them as instances of one overarching symbolic logic rather than disconnected metaphors.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book synthesizes the primary and alternative meanings provided by the consensus into a single, broader functional definition, retaining the same referents but elevating their underlying conceptual unity.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The claim that all uses of the net imply a sorting where the catch is 'never ransomed' or 'wholly consecrated or wholly destroyed' relies heavily on the Hebrew linguistic connection between 'dragnet' and the 'dev